## How the merge was made

owl's main was squashed on 2026-11-11, so it shares no ancestor with
upstream and `git merge upstream/main` cannot be used. The merge was a
three-way merge over transformed trees:

    B' = T(e17b6cefc)                orphan commit
    U' = T(upstream/main)            parent B'
    O' = owl's tree at 1bf8fec80     parent B'
    merge U' into O'                 conflicts resolved by ownership

T is `scripts/upstream-sync/rename.py`. It renders an upstream tree in
owl's naming: `github.com/yetone/magpie` → `github.com/istoo/owl`,
`MAGPIE_` → `OWL_`, `Magpie`/`magpie` → `Owl`/`owl`, path components
included, and re-runs gofmt because a shorter struct field realigns the
fields after it.

What T leaves alone (the compatibility boundary, not owl's name to change):

    @magpie-community/...      the community plugins' npm scope
    magpie-community/...       the community plugins repository
    X-Magpie-*, x-magpie-*     gateway request headers
    json:"magpie"              a plugin package.json's manifest key
    "magpie": ...              that key in JSON text, and manifest access
                               (`?.magpie?`, `["magpie"]`) in plugin code
    magpie_label, magpie_by, magpie_price, magpie_via,
    magpie_cache_unknown, magpie_deferred
                               old JSON fields the catalog and gateway read
    "magpie":"routing-group"   a shared routing group's format marker
    usemagpie                  the upstream site; owl removes those calls
                               itself, and leaving them visible is the point

Calibration: T(e17b6cefc) against owl's 68bbe1383 leaves 848 differing
files, every remaining `magpie` token in T's tree is one of the exceptions
above, and the files containing one are the plugin subsystem's. The
remaining differences are owl's own work (UI, memory, backports), not
missed or wrong renames.

What the 2026-11-17 merge had to fix by hand, and the next one will meet
again:

    fixed-column help text  a rename shortens "magpie" to "owl" by three,
                            and every table aligned to the old width
                            (internal/cli/model_cli.go's modelUsage and its
                            spec and api siblings) is three columns off.
                            Realign them to the column owl's name gives.
    com.yetone.magpie.*     the transform leaves com.yetone, so the new
                            ccswitch scheme came out com.yetone.owl.*.
                            Owl's are com.istoo.owl.* (Info.plist, daemon).
    lengths                 upstream code that writes over an id "at one
                            length" (Codex's model_provider, openai and
                            magpie both six) is wrong for owl's three:
                            relabel pads or rewrites a whole file instead.
    the word, not letters   a guard that greps for the project's name finds
                            the letters inside ordinary words once the name
                            is three letters ("knowledge" has owl). Match a
                            word boundary, and check every i18n key and test
                            comment that names the old project.
    owl's split files       owl moved parts of app.js out (drawEditor to
                            editor.js, usage packing to
                            plugins/usage/page.js). A merge that keeps owl's
                            side of that region also keeps its older copy
                            and silently drops upstream's new blocks. Merge
                            the moved function against B' and U' as its own
                            three-way diff (editor.js needed 75 lines back),
                            and delete the same declarations from app.js.
    two of one thing        both sides' implementations can end up beside
                            each other: app.js and page.js each declared
                            usagePacked (the page's script then threw
                            "already declared" and drew nothing), and
                            decide_group.go masked a decision twice. A page
                            that loads or a package that builds is no proof:
                            run the GUI file and the package's tests.
    owl's fast paths        upstream rewrites a function owl had optimized
                            (modelOf's strings.Clone, withFields' top-level
                            reader, uploadLanes, redactionWanted's
                            fail-closed read). Take owl's version back and
                            keep upstream's new behaviour around it.

## The next sync

1. `git fetch upstream`, record the new sha here.
2. Unpack `git archive <sha>` and run `rename.py` over it.
3. Build B' as `T(<the sha this file last names>)` if it is not in history:
   it was written down here only, so keep the transformed tree of the
   previous upstream commit, or redo the calibration against a known owl
   commit.
4. Merge `T(<new sha>)` into owl's tree with B' as the base.
5. Resolve conflicts: owl's own files win (its release chain, README,
   .github/workflows, build/, docs/owl-design.md, TASKS.md, the tray
   panel, the memory work); everything else takes upstream.
6. Read the hand fixes above first; grep the merged tree for
   "usemagpie" in runtime code, `com.yetone.owl`, help tables whose
   continuations sit at an older column, and `\bowl\b` in guards that
   meant the old name. Diff every file owl had split out (editor.js,
   plugins/usage/page.js) against B' and U', and run a page that loads
   app.js before trusting a merge: a repeated top-level `let` throws at
   once and nothing else on the page runs.